171
00:15:19.576 --> 00:15:27.363
<v Maya>Yes. Adding that one supported context caused performance to skyrocket. Depth 5 nesting jumped from 19 out of 24 to a perfect 24 out of 24.</v>

172
00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:32.531
<v Daniel>Wait, really? Just from one example?</v>

173
00:15:32.571 --> 00:15:40.315
<v Maya>Yeah. And for length 8 ordering, adding that single supported context lifted the score from absolute 0 up to 13 out of 24.</v>

174
00:15:40.695 --> 00:15:45.198
<v Daniel>That proves that the failure isn't a lack of raw cognitive capacity in the latent space.</v>

175
00:15:45.318 --> 00:15:52.782
<v Maya>Exactly. The limiting factor is the coverage in the examples provided. If the context gives it permission to extrapolate that far, the model can do it.</v>

176
00:15:53.312 --> 00:15:57.234
<v Daniel>And the actual visual signatures of those failures are incredibly telling too.</v>

177
00:15:57.314 --> 00:15:58.734
<v Maya>Yes, they look very different.</v>

178
00:15:58.915 --> 00:16:04.817
<v Daniel>Like when the model fails at ordering, the output grid just becomes a chaotic mess, right? The dimensions are totally wrecked.</v>

179
00:16:04.957 --> 00:16:05.978
<v Maya>Completely wrecked.</v>

180
00:16:06.238 --> 00:16:13.461
<v Daniel>But when nesting fails, the model keeps the perfect visual structure of the shapes and just makes one tiny localized relational error.</v>

181
00:16:13.901 --> 00:16:19.624
<v Maya>It understands the grammar of the nested shapes, but it loses its spatial mapping when the sequence gets too complex.</v>

182
00:16:19.938 --> 00:16:25.421
<v Daniel>Okay, but what happens when you combine rules? Because the complexity has to multiply when we look at composition.</v>

183
00:16:25.741 --> 00:16:35.285
<v Maya>It does, but you might assume the difficulty just stacks linearly. Composition actually proves to be highly representation dependent. It's not a single scalar difficulty.</v>

184
00:16:35.666 --> 00:16:38.347
<v Daniel>Give us an example of how that representation dependency plays out.</v>

185
00:16:38.565 --> 00:16:42.969
<v Maya>Let's take three independent skills, relocation, reflection, and rotation.</v>

186
00:16:43.249 --> 00:16:43.469
<v Daniel>Okay.</v>

187
00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:48.433
<v Maya>The model can solve 72 out of 72 tasks for each of those alone.</v>

188
00:16:48.774 --> 00:16:49.434
<v Daniel>Perfect score.</v>

189
00:16:49.775 --> 00:16:56.660
<v Maya>Right. And if you compose rotation with relocation, asking it to spin a shape and move it, it stays flawless at 72 out of 72.</v>

190
00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:57.181
<v Daniel>Makes sense.</v>

191
00:16:57.821 --> 00:17:04.527
<v Maya>But if you compose reflection with relocation, asking it to flip a shape and move it, performance drops to 47 out of 72.</v>

192
00:17:04.667 --> 00:17:08.010
<v Daniel>So it loses the ability to perform those operations simultaneously.</v>

193
00:17:08.325 --> 00:17:10.967
<v Maya>It gets worse. The most extreme example is color swapping.</v>

194
00:17:11.547 --> 00:17:12.228
<v Daniel>Okay. What happens there?</v>

195
00:17:12.848 --> 00:17:20.614
<v Maya>On its own, the model only solves 26 out of 72 color swap tasks when you ask it to compose color swapping with relocation.</v>

196
00:17:21.234 --> 00:17:25.077
<v Daniel>It solves zero out of 72. It completely breaks down.</v>

197
00:17:25.137 --> 00:17:33.963
<v Maya>Zero. Wow. Before we analyze what these breakdowns mean for AI intelligence as a whole, we should definitely credit the rigorous methodology of this paper.</v>

198
00:17:33.983 --> 00:17:35.925
<v Daniel>Oh, absolutely. The rigor is worth crediting.</v>

199
00:17:36.179 --> 00:17:42.540
<v Maya>The authors had co-authors from Beelick AI and NYU conduct an independent black box audit.</v>

200
00:17:42.801 --> 00:17:48.082
<v Daniel>Yes, they reproduced that 29.5% score without ever having access to the model weights.</v>

201
00:17:48.302 --> 00:17:53.323
<v Maya>Which is crucial for trust in these numbers. They also performed an opaque identifier application.</v>

202
00:17:53.443 --> 00:18:02.405
<v Daniel>Right, by hiding the semantic tags of the tasks, they proved the system wasn't exploiting descriptive task IDs or concept-degrouped bashes to cheat.</v>

203
00:18:02.794 --> 00:18:09.496
<v Maya>It scored 96 out of 160 with opaque IDs versus 95 with semantic ones, so virtually identical.</v>

204
00:18:09.776 --> 00:18:15.098
<v Daniel>Furthermore, repeated requests yielded bite-identical results, proving the latent reasoning is perfectly deterministic.</v>

205
00:18:15.378 --> 00:18:24.321
<v Maya>So the scientific rigor is unimpeachable. They proved the model isn't cheating via IDs, and they transparently mapped exactly where its reasoning fractures.</v>

206
00:18:24.652 --> 00:18:39.640
<v Daniel>But zooming out, we have to confront the sharpest tension in this entire evaluation. We do. Is a tiny, highly efficient model scoring 29.5% on ARC actually demonstrating generalized intelligence? Or is it just a highly optimized pattern</v>

207
00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:40.000
<v Daniel>mantra?</v>

208
00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:46.763
<v Maya>To answer that, we turn to our final background paper, Francois Chalet's 2019 treatise on the measure of intelligence.</v>

209
00:18:47.364 --> 00:18:49.965
<v Daniel>And Chalet actually created the ARC benchmark.</v>

210
00:18:50.145 --> 00:18:53.727
<v Maya>Right. And he defines intelligence as skill acquisition efficiency.</v>

211
00:18:54.074 --> 00:18:57.278
<v Daniel>So it is not about the breadth of what a system already knows.</v>

212
00:18:57.378 --> 00:19:03.905
<v Maya>No, it is about how efficiently that system can learn a brand new skill from sparse, unfamiliar examples.</v>

213
00:19:04.126 --> 00:19:06.468
<v Daniel>And Chile issued a very specific warning in 2019.</v>

214
00:19:07.233 --> 00:19:17.536
<v Maya>He argued that if you give a model massive amounts of specific training data or unlimited priors, you can buy skill in a way that perfectly masks actual generalization.</v>

215
00:19:17.676 --> 00:19:24.377
<v Daniel>The model looks like it is reasoning on the fly, but it is really just retrieving deeply ingrained patterns from its massive training set.</v>

216
00:19:24.478 --> 00:19:24.898
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

217
00:19:24.918 --> 00:19:33.720
<v Daniel>Let's point Chile as warning directly at BDHCQ. The authors are very open that BDHCQ trains on a highly curated proprietary ARC style mixture.</v>

218
00:19:34.178 --> 00:19:44.223
<v Maya>very curated. They fed it the RCGI-1 training set, RER, ConceptArc, ArseHeavy, RCRN, 100K, plus private data.</v>

219
00:19:44.724 --> 00:19:47.905
<v Daniel>The model is absolutely marinated in ARC-shaped puzzles.</v>

220
00:19:48.105 --> 00:19:48.525
<v Maya>It is.</v>

221
00:19:48.846 --> 00:19:56.830
<v Daniel>So the critical question remains, is a cheap 29.5% score actually skill acquisition efficiency in Cholet's sense?</v>

222
00:19:57.110 --> 00:20:00.432
<v Maya>Or is it just skill bought with a mountain of highly specific data?</v>

223
00:20:00.532 --> 00:20:00.712
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

224
00:20:01.247 --> 00:20:09.884
<v Maya>Well, the honest evidence points heavily toward the skeptical reading. And to the author's immense credit, they provided the exact evidence required to make that critique.</v>

225
00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.972
<v Daniel>You mean the paper's own consistency gap and the composition failures?</v>

226
00:20:13.072 --> 00:20:24.818
<v Maya>Yes. The failure to apply a recognized rule across all test inputs in a single task and its bizarre failures in composing simple rules. It indicates it hasn't fully induced the abstract logic of reasoning.</v>

227
00:20:25.058 --> 00:20:28.680
<v Daniel>It has basically just learned the visual statistical patterns of A or C puzzles.</v>

228
00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:36.164
<v Maya>Exactly. It resembles a student who has memorized thousands of specific IQ test questions rather than actually increasing their fluid intelligence.</v>

229
00:20:36.472 --> 00:20:49.800
<v Daniel>So the team at Pathway and their collaborators engineered a vastly cheaper, incredibly innovative reasoning engine. But the fuel they poured into that engine to achieve this 29.5% result was heavily skewed toward the specific test it was taking.</v>

230
00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:57.364
<v Maya>But here's the thing. While the training data was hyper-specific, we cannot dismiss what the BDH architecture itself has achieved.</v>

231
00:20:57.744 --> 00:20:58.945
<v Daniel>Because of the cost efficiency.</v>

232
00:20:59.485 --> 00:21:03.788
<v Maya>Right. Cost efficiency might be the axis that truly matters as we move forward.</v>

233
00:21:04.777 --> 00:21:13.881
<v Daniel>Since we are in an era where raw accuracy on benchmarks is increasingly just bought with massive unsustainable amounts of computing power.</v>

234
00:21:14.021 --> 00:21:23.985
<v Maya>Exactly. A 150 million parameter model breaking a cost frontier says something very real and very disruptive about the potential of latent reasoning architectures.</v>

235
00:21:24.326 --> 00:21:28.447
<v Daniel>Even if its current performance relies heavily on ARC specific data.</v>

236
00:21:28.667 --> 00:21:28.948
<v Maya>Right.</v>

237
00:21:29.208 --> 00:21:34.610
<v Daniel>So it represents a massive leap in how models compute, even if the depth of what they're computing is still limited.</v>

238
00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:35.611
<v Maya>That's a great way to put it.</v>

239
00:21:35.711 --> 00:21:39.375
<v Daniel>We should probably recap the honest limits that the authors themselves acknowledge.</v>

240
00:21:39.395 --> 00:21:46.682
<v Maya>We should. First, the internal training recipe is proprietary, meaning we can't look under the hood to see the exact data mixture.</v>

241
00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:53.556
<v Daniel>Right. They also haven't even attempted the much harder ARC-AGI-2 benchmark yet. It's completely untouched.</v>

242
00:21:53.776 --> 00:22:01.403
<v Maya>And fundamentally, a system that only solves two out of three test inputs on a puzzle has not actually mastered rule induction.</v>

243
00:22:01.704 --> 00:22:07.669
<v Daniel>So it is a significant architectural milestone, not the finish line for artificial general intelligence.</v>

244
00:22:07.689 --> 00:22:08.910
<v Maya>Definitely not the finish line.</v>

245
00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:11.032
<v Daniel>Which leaves us with a pretty provocative final thought.</v>

246
00:22:11.433 --> 00:22:11.513
<v Maya>Oh.</v>

247
00:22:11.973 --> 00:22:16.315
<v Daniel>Yeah, we started by picturing the chess grandmaster making a devastating move in total silence.</v>

248
00:22:16.556 --> 00:22:16.836
<v Maya>Right.</v>

249
00:22:17.396 --> 00:22:29.783
<v Daniel>If AI models continue to move toward this latent reasoning paradigm, you know, thinking in continuous high dimensional spaces rather than English words, what does that mean for our ability to control them?</v>

250
00:22:30.063 --> 00:22:31.163
<v Maya>That is the big question.</v>

251
00:22:31.424 --> 00:22:39.608
<v Daniel>Currently, we rely heavily on chain of thought to monitor AI. We force it to talk to itself so we can read its logic and catch its mistakes before it acts.</v>

252
00:22:39.929 --> 00:22:46.555
<v Maya>But if the most efficient, most powerful future models think entirely in an untranslatable, nonverbal mathematical space.</v>

253
00:22:46.635 --> 00:22:48.296
<v Daniel>How will we ever audit their reasoning?</v>

254
00:22:48.637 --> 00:22:53.921
<v Maya>How will we ever know what they are truly thinking in the silence before they make their move?</v>

255
00:22:54.241 --> 00:22:59.446
<v Daniel>It poses a profound structural challenge for AI safety and interpretability moving forward.</v>

256
00:22:59.546 --> 00:23:00.227
<v Maya>It really does.</v>

257
00:23:00.287 --> 00:23:01.067
<v Daniel>Something to mull over.</v>

258
00:23:01.228 --> 00:23:01.428
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

259
00:23:01.848 --> 00:23:07.213
<v Daniel>So keep questioning the data. Keep looking behind the efficiency curves. And thank you for taking this deep dive with us.</v>
